window.lectureContent = {"10":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH COURSE  •  FIRST-YEAR UNIVERSITY  •  UNIT 10","Management Concepts and Styles","Control • planning • staffing • persuasive, democratic, laissez-faire styles • conditionals","Effective management aligns clear goals with people, processes, and action.","LEARN  •  DISCUSS  •  APPLY","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 10  •  LEARNING MAP","Management Concepts and Styles","Choose a section to jump to, or follow the lesson with NEXT.","01","FUNCTIONS","02","STYLES","03","LANGUAGE","04","CASE","For clickable navigation, present the deck in Slide Show mode.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["LEARNING OBJECTIVES","By the end of this lecture, you can…","1","Explain control, planning, and staffing as management activities.","2","Compare three leadership styles and when each may fit.","3","Use conditional sentences to discuss likely outcomes and general principles.","4","Recommend a management approach for a workplace scenario.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  03","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["OPENING QUESTION","THINK • VOTE • EXPLAIN","Your team has missed a deadline","A project is late. The team says the goal was unclear, tasks were unrealistic, and no one knew who owned the final check. What should the manager address first?","A","Control and measures","B","Planning and responsibilities","C","Staffing and training","30 seconds think • 60 seconds pair discussion • whole-class vote","There may be more than one defensible answer—support yours with a reason.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  04","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["CORE CONCEPTS","The essential ideas","CONTROL","Clarify goals, measures, responsibilities, and progress.","PLANNING","Select objectives and coordinate who does what, when, and where; it continues during implementation.","STAFFING","Place suitable people in roles, develop skills, and retain talent.","LEADERSHIP","Choose how to involve, guide, and empower the team in context.","Check: explain one card to a partner without reading it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  05","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["COMPARE & CONNECT","Three styles: different levels of involvement","PERSUASIVE / DEMOCRATIC","Persuasive: explain, model, and build buy-in.","Democratic: involve staff in decisions.","Use clear processes to keep participation efficient.","LAISSEZ-FAIRE","Manager acts more like a mentor.","Employees have autonomy to decide.","Works best with capable, self-directed teams and clear boundaries.","Pair-share: give a real example of each side of this comparison.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  06","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["ACADEMIC VOCABULARY","Terms you will use in this unit","objective","specific result to achieve","implementation","putting a plan into action","retain","keep valued employees","incentive","reward that encourages an action","buy-in","team acceptance of a direction","empower","give people authority and confidence to act","Active recall: cover the right-hand side and define each term aloud.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  07","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["LANGUAGE FOCUS","Conditionals: connect conditions and outcomes","Zero: If + present, present (general result). First: If + present, will + base verb (likely future result).","If goals are clear, teams work more consistently. (zero)","If the team shares ideas, the manager will make a better-informed decision. (first)","Avoid “will” in the if-clause: If the plan changes, we will review it.","Notice the form • identify the meaning • create a new example.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  08","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["GUIDED PRACTICE","Try first; then check","1. If a manager invites input, employees often ______ more ownership. (feel)","feel","2. If planning continues during implementation, the team ______ risks earlier. (identify)","will identify","3. If the team ______ clear measures, it will track progress. (have)","has","4. Complete: If employees are empowered, ______.","Possible: they may make decisions faster / they will take more ownership","Suggested answers are shown for self-check after discussion.","Teacher move: ask students to justify one answer, not only supply it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  09","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["FORMATIVE CHECK","Pause and retrieve","1","Does planning stop when implementation begins?","CHECK: No","2","Which style explicitly invites participation in decisions?","CHECK: Democratic","3","Complete: If a manager ___ the team, employees will contribute ideas.","CHECK: consults / involves","Students answer on paper / mini-whiteboards before revealing the check.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  10","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["COLLABORATIVE APPLICATION","Choose a style for the moment","A skilled design team needs to create a new campaign quickly. The deadline is fixed, but team members have different specialist knowledge.","1","Choose a leadership style or a blend.","2","Explain one benefit and one risk.","3","Write two conditional sentences describing expected outcomes.","Roles: facilitator • vocabulary tracker • reporter","Report one conclusion and one question your group still has.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  11","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":12,"paragraphs":["EXIT TICKET","Three things to take away","1","Control clarifies progress; planning coordinates action; staffing supports people.","2","No one style fits every task; context and capability matter.","3","Zero and first conditionals express general and likely outcomes.","Before you leave: write one concept, one new term, and one question.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 10  •  12","MAP","BACK"]}],"11":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH COURSE  •  FIRST-YEAR UNIVERSITY  •  UNIT 11","Five Basic Parts of an Organization","Organizational structure • coordination • job design • relative clauses","Structure is a way to coordinate work, not an end in itself.","LEARN  •  DISCUSS  •  APPLY","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 11  •  LEARNING MAP","Five Basic Parts of an Organization","Choose a section to jump to, or follow the lesson with NEXT.","01","STRUCTURE","02","COORDINATION","03","LANGUAGE","04","APPLY","For clickable navigation, present the deck in Slide Show mode.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["LEARNING OBJECTIVES","By the end of this lecture, you can…","1","Identify the five parts of an organization.","2","Explain mutual adjustment, supervision, and standardization.","3","Distinguish job enlargement from job enrichment.","4","Use relative clauses to define organizational roles and units.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  03","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["OPENING QUESTION","THINK • VOTE • EXPLAIN","A growing bakery needs coordination","A small bakery hires more staff, opens another branch, and introduces quality checks. What new roles or systems will help work stay coordinated?","A","More supervision","B","Standard procedures and analysts","C","Both, depending on the work","30 seconds think • 60 seconds pair discussion • whole-class vote","There may be more than one defensible answer—support yours with a reason.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  04","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["CORE CONCEPTS","The essential ideas","STRATEGIC APEX","Leaders who set overall direction.","MIDDLE LINE","Managers who connect senior leaders with operations.","OPERATING CORE","Workers who produce goods or deliver services.","TECHNOSTRUCTURE + SUPPORT","Analysts standardize work; support staff provide services that enable operations.","Check: explain one card to a partner without reading it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  05","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["COMPARE & CONNECT","Five parts, one coordinated organization","COORDINATE THE WORK","Mutual adjustment: operators coordinate directly.","Direct supervision: a manager gives direction.","Standardization: processes, outputs, or skills follow agreed rules.","DESIGN THE JOB","Enlargement adds more tasks (horizontal breadth).","Enrichment gives more control or responsibility (vertical depth).","Job design can influence autonomy and motivation.","Pair-share: give a real example of each side of this comparison.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  06","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["ACADEMIC VOCABULARY","Terms you will use in this unit","operating core","people who do the organization’s basic work","strategic apex","top leadership and its staff","middle line","managers linking the top and operations","analyst","specialist who standardizes or studies work","standardization","coordination through agreed procedures or results","mutual adjustment","coordination through direct communication","Active recall: cover the right-hand side and define each term aloud.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  07","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["LANGUAGE FOCUS","Relative clauses identify people and things","who = people; which = things; that = people or things in defining clauses; where = places.","Operators are employees who do the basic work.","Standardization is a method that coordinates work.","A support unit is a part of the organization which provides internal services.","Notice the form • identify the meaning • create a new example.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  08","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["GUIDED PRACTICE","Try first; then check","1. Analysts are specialists ______ standardize processes. (who/which)","who","2. A procedure is a rule ______ guides a task. (who/that)","that / which","3. The operating core is the group ______ produces the service. (who/where)","that / which","4. Join: A manager coordinates a team. The manager supervises operators.","A manager is a person who coordinates a team and supervises operators.","Suggested answers are shown for self-check after discussion.","Teacher move: ask students to justify one answer, not only supply it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  09","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["FORMATIVE CHECK","Pause and retrieve","1","Which part performs the basic production work?","CHECK: Operating core","2","Who links the strategic apex to the operating core?","CHECK: Middle line","3","More tasks = enlargement or enrichment?","CHECK: Job enlargement","Students answer on paper / mini-whiteboards before revealing the check.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  10","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["COLLABORATIVE APPLICATION","Map a university department","Map a department using the five-part model. Include lecturers, department leadership, administrative support, and staff who create procedures or quality standards.","1","Place each role in a structural part.","2","Choose a coordination mechanism for one task.","3","Write two relative-clause definitions of roles.","Roles: facilitator • vocabulary tracker • reporter","Report one conclusion and one question your group still has.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  11","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":12,"paragraphs":["EXIT TICKET","Three things to take away","1","Structure divides and coordinates work across five organizational parts.","2","Analysts standardize processes, outputs, or skills; support units enable the organization.","3","Enlargement adds tasks; enrichment adds control; relative clauses define roles.","Before you leave: write one concept, one new term, and one question.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 11  •  12","MAP","BACK"]}],"12":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH COURSE  •  FIRST-YEAR UNIVERSITY  •  UNIT 12","Organizational Culture","Shared values • culture content and strength • perfect tenses","Culture is visible in repeated behaviors, shared assumptions, and everyday choices.","LEARN  •  DISCUSS  •  APPLY","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 12  •  LEARNING MAP","Organizational Culture","Choose a section to jump to, or follow the lesson with NEXT.","01","MEANING","02","INFLUENCES","03","LANGUAGE","04","CASE","For clickable navigation, present the deck in Slide Show mode.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["LEARNING OBJECTIVES","By the end of this lecture, you can…","1","Define organizational culture and distinguish content from strength.","2","Explain how structure, leaders, and the business environment shape culture.","3","Use present perfect and past perfect to express time relationships.","4","Diagnose cultural signals in a short organization case.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  03","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["OPENING QUESTION","THINK • VOTE • EXPLAIN","Values on the wall—or values at work?","A company poster says “We value initiative,” but every decision must be approved by senior managers. What might employees experience?","A","A strong initiative culture","B","A gap between stated values and practice","C","No organizational culture","30 seconds think • 60 seconds pair discussion • whole-class vote","There may be more than one defensible answer—support yours with a reason.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  04","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["CORE CONCEPTS","The essential ideas","CULTURE CONTENT","The shared assumptions, values, and priorities that guide behavior.","CULTURE STRENGTH","How widely and intensely members share and enact assumptions.","CLIMATE","The felt atmosphere employees experience in daily work.","CULTURE SIGNALS","Decisions, routines, rewards, structures, and customer-service practices.","Check: explain one card to a partner without reading it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  05","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["COMPARE & CONNECT","How culture is shaped—and what it shapes","CULTURE IS INFLUENCED BY","Industry and business environment","Leaders’ vision and strategies","Formal structures, plans, and policies","CULTURE INFLUENCES","Employee initiative and ownership","Cooperation and readiness to change","Customer service and organizational behavior","Pair-share: give a real example of each side of this comparison.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  06","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["ACADEMIC VOCABULARY","Terms you will use in this unit","shared values","beliefs held in common by members","assumption","basic belief treated as true","initiative","ability to act independently","ownership","feeling personally invested in outcomes","persist","continue over time","enforce","make a rule or decision take effect","Active recall: cover the right-hand side and define each term aloud.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  07","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["LANGUAGE FOCUS","Perfect tenses: connect events across time","Present perfect: have/has + past participle (past action linked to now). Past perfect: had + past participle (earlier past action).","The company has developed a strong service culture. (relevant now)","By 2024, the firm had introduced team-based decision-making. (before a past time)","Shared values have influenced daily routines.","Notice the form • identify the meaning • create a new example.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  08","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["GUIDED PRACTICE","Try first; then check","1. The organization ______ a new mentoring culture over time. (develop — present perfect)","has developed","2. By the time the new CEO arrived, staff ______ the old routines. (learn — past perfect)","had learned","3. Leaders ______ the values in hiring decisions. (reinforce — present perfect)","have reinforced","Suggested answers are shown for self-check after discussion.","Teacher move: ask students to justify one answer, not only supply it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  09","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["FORMATIVE CHECK","Pause and retrieve","1","What is the difference between culture content and strength?","CHECK: Content = which assumptions/values; strength = how widely/intensely shared","2","Name one way leaders shape culture.","CHECK: Vision, strategy, decisions, policies, or example","3","Choose: By 2023, the firm (has / had) changed its policy.","CHECK: had","Students answer on paper / mini-whiteboards before revealing the check.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  10","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["COLLABORATIVE APPLICATION","Culture audit: evidence, not slogans","A technology firm says it values learning and cooperation. New employees report little training, and teams rarely share information.","1","Identify the stated values and observed behaviors.","2","Name two evidence sources you would examine.","3","Recommend one structural or practical change and explain its likely cultural signal.","Roles: facilitator • vocabulary tracker • reporter","Report one conclusion and one question your group still has.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  11","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":12,"paragraphs":["EXIT TICKET","Three things to take away","1","Culture consists of shared assumptions and behavior patterns; content differs from strength.","2","Environment, leaders, structures, and practices shape culture; culture also shapes behavior.","3","Use have/has + participle for relevance now; had + participle for an earlier past event.","Before you leave: write one concept, one new term, and one question.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 12  •  12","MAP","BACK"]}],"13":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH COURSE  •  FIRST-YEAR UNIVERSITY  •  UNIT 13","The Banking Industry","Retail and investment banks • lending risk • central banks • perfect tenses","Banks connect savers, borrowers, businesses, and financial markets.","LEARN  •  DISCUSS  •  APPLY","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 13  •  LEARNING MAP","The Banking Industry","Choose a section to jump to, or follow the lesson with NEXT.","01","BANK TYPES","02","LENDING","03","LANGUAGE","04","CASE","For clickable navigation, present the deck in Slide Show mode.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["LEARNING OBJECTIVES","By the end of this lecture, you can…","1","Describe the main functions of commercial and investment banks.","2","Explain the interest spread, collateral, liquidity, and creditworthiness.","3","Distinguish central-bank rates from lending rates to customers.","4","Use perfect tenses to describe banking developments and experiences.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  03","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["OPENING QUESTION","THINK • VOTE • EXPLAIN","A bank has deposits—but also loan requests","A small business applies for a loan. The bank must consider repayment risk, liquidity, and the return it may earn. What information should it examine?","A","Only the requested amount","B","Income, credit standing, security, and cash needs","C","Only the owner’s age","30 seconds think • 60 seconds pair discussion • whole-class vote","There may be more than one defensible answer—support yours with a reason.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  04","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["CORE CONCEPTS","The essential ideas","COMMERCIAL / RETAIL BANK","Receives deposits, processes payments, lends, and may exchange currency.","INVESTMENT BANK","Raises funds, underwrites securities, advises on mergers, and provides market services.","UNIVERSAL BANK","Combines commercial and investment services in one institution.","BANK PROFIT","Commercial banks may earn a spread between deposit and lending rates; investment banks often earn fees and commissions.","Check: explain one card to a partner without reading it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  05","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["COMPARE & CONNECT","Lending balances return, risk, and liquidity","BORROWER ASSESSMENT","Creditworthiness estimates ability to repay.","Collateral is an asset pledged as security.","Risk and expected yield are assessed together.","BANKING SYSTEM","Central bank sets a policy / discount rate for lending to banks.","Commercial banks set customer rates based on costs and risk.","Liquidity means having funds available when needed.","Pair-share: give a real example of each side of this comparison.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  06","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["ACADEMIC VOCABULARY","Terms you will use in this unit","deposit","money placed in an account","spread / margin","difference between rates paid and charged","liquidity","availability of cash or readily usable funds","collateral","asset used to secure a loan","solvency","ability to meet financial obligations","Eurocurrency","currency deposited outside its country of origin","Active recall: cover the right-hand side and define each term aloud.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  07","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["LANGUAGE FOCUS","Perfect tenses: describe banking time relationships","Present perfect: have/has + past participle. Past perfect: had + past participle. Use time markers carefully.","The bank has expanded its digital services. (change relevant now)","The borrower has provided collateral. (completed; current result)","The central bank had lowered the rate before lending increased. (earlier past event)","Notice the form • identify the meaning • create a new example.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  08","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["GUIDED PRACTICE","Try first; then check","1. The borrower ______ the required documents. (submit — present perfect)","has submitted","2. Banks ______ credit as loans circulate through accounts. (create — present perfect)","have created","3. Before the rules changed, the two activities ______ separate. (remain — past perfect)","had remained","Suggested answers are shown for self-check after discussion.","Teacher move: ask students to justify one answer, not only supply it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  09","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["FORMATIVE CHECK","Pause and retrieve","1","What is collateral?","CHECK: An asset used to secure a loan","2","How may a retail bank earn a spread?","CHECK: Charge borrowers a higher rate than it pays depositors, subject to risk and costs","3","Eurocurrency is defined by location or currency type?","CHECK: Location: a currency held outside its country of origin","Students answer on paper / mini-whiteboards before revealing the check.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  10","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["COLLABORATIVE APPLICATION","Loan committee simulation","A local company requests financing to buy equipment. It offers a building as security, but its cash flow varies by season.","1","Identify two factors the bank should assess.","2","Explain the role of collateral and liquidity.","3","Draft two sentences using a perfect tense to report information received.","Roles: facilitator • vocabulary tracker • reporter","Report one conclusion and one question your group still has.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  11","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":12,"paragraphs":["EXIT TICKET","Three things to take away","1","Retail, investment, and universal banks provide different combinations of services.","2","Creditworthiness, collateral, liquidity, and yield shape lending decisions.","3","Perfect tenses connect completed banking actions with now or with another past event.","Before you leave: write one concept, one new term, and one question.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 13  •  12","MAP","BACK"]}],"14":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH COURSE  •  FIRST-YEAR UNIVERSITY  •  UNIT 14","Marketing","Customer needs • marketing mix • segmentation and research • infinitives of purpose","Marketing starts with understanding a target market and creating value for it.","LEARN  •  DISCUSS  •  APPLY","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 14  •  LEARNING MAP","Marketing","Choose a section to jump to, or follow the lesson with NEXT.","01","NEEDS","02","THE 4Ps","03","LANGUAGE","04","CAMPAIGN","For clickable navigation, present the deck in Slide Show mode.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["LEARNING OBJECTIVES","By the end of this lecture, you can…","1","Define marketing as a process for identifying and satisfying needs.","2","Explain the 4Ps of the marketing mix (and packaging as an added P).","3","Distinguish the marketing concept from the selling concept.","4","Use infinitives of purpose to explain marketing actions.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  03","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["OPENING QUESTION","THINK • VOTE • EXPLAIN","A useful product—but no buyers","A student team has designed a reusable bottle but has not asked potential customers what they need or what they will pay. What should the team do before launching?","A","Produce as many as possible","B","Research the target market","C","Spend the whole budget on advertising","30 seconds think • 60 seconds pair discussion • whole-class vote","There may be more than one defensible answer—support yours with a reason.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  04","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["CORE CONCEPTS","The essential ideas","PRODUCT","What to offer: features, quality, design, and benefits.","PRICE","What to charge: price level, discounts, and payment terms.","PLACE","Where and how customers can access the offer.","PROMOTION","How to communicate value; packaging is sometimes treated as an added P.","Check: explain one card to a partner without reading it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  05","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["COMPARE & CONNECT","Marketing concept vs selling concept","SELLING CONCEPT","Starts with an existing product.","Uses strong promotion to persuade purchase.","Focuses on selling what the producer has made.","MARKETING CONCEPT","Starts with customer needs and target markets.","Uses research to guide the offer.","Aims to make what customers are likely to value.","Pair-share: give a real example of each side of this comparison.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  06","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["ACADEMIC VOCABULARY","Terms you will use in this unit","target market","group of consumers a product is aimed at","market research","collecting and analyzing customer information","segmentation","dividing a market into meaningful groups","feature","distinctive product characteristic","benefit","value or advantage for a customer","marketing mix","combined product, price, place, and promotion decisions","Active recall: cover the right-hand side and define each term aloud.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  07","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["LANGUAGE FOCUS","Infinitives of purpose: explain why","to + base verb answers “why?” and expresses purpose.","Companies use advertising to support their products.","Marketers study competitors to identify their strengths.","Firms collect consumer data to analyze market reactions.","Notice the form • identify the meaning • create a new example.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  08","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["GUIDED PRACTICE","Try first; then check","1. Managers segment markets. They want to identify opportunities. → Managers segment markets ______.","to identify opportunities","2. Companies research customers. They want to understand needs. → Companies research customers ______.","to understand needs","3. Firms design packaging. They want to protect the product. → Firms design packaging ______.","to protect the product","Suggested answers are shown for self-check after discussion.","Teacher move: ask students to justify one answer, not only supply it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  09","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["FORMATIVE CHECK","Pause and retrieve","1","What are the four Ps?","CHECK: Product, price, place, promotion","2","Why is market research safer than guesswork?","CHECK: It uses evidence about market size and customer reactions","3","Complete: A firm conducts research ___ identify customer needs.","CHECK: to","Students answer on paper / mini-whiteboards before revealing the check.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  10","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["COLLABORATIVE APPLICATION","Build a mini marketing mix","Create a campaign for a low-cost campus meal plan aimed at first-year students.","1","Define one target segment and a need.","2","Choose product, price, place, and promotion decisions.","3","Write one infinitive-of-purpose sentence for two decisions.","Roles: facilitator • vocabulary tracker • reporter","Report one conclusion and one question your group still has.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  11","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":12,"paragraphs":["EXIT TICKET","Three things to take away","1","Marketing identifies needs and creates value for a target market.","2","The 4Ps work as a connected mix; market research reduces reliance on guesswork.","3","Use to + base verb to explain purpose.","Before you leave: write one concept, one new term, and one question.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 14  •  12","MAP","BACK"]}],"1":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH 1     •     UNIT 01","Academic Language","Formal vocabulary • style • meaning in context","INTERACTIVE CLASSROOM PRESENTATION","Discuss  •  Practise  •  Apply","01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 01  •  LEARNING MAP","Academic Language","Choose a section to jump in, or use NEXT to follow the lesson.","01","WORD CHOICE","Formal or informal?","02","ACADEMIC STYLE","Make writing precise","03","CONTEXT","One word, new meaning","04","PRACTISE","Try the challenge","Tip: Switch to Slide Show mode and click a section card.","02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["TODAY’S LEARNING GOALS","By the end of this lesson, you can…","1","Distinguish formal and informal vocabulary.","2","Choose precise academic alternatives.","3","Use context to identify a word’s meaning.","4","Write short, appropriate academic sentences.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","03"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["WORD CHOICE","Academic words make meaning precise","Match each everyday word with its academic alternative.","get","→","receive / obtain / achieve","try","→","attempt","use","→","employ","ways","→","strategies","right","→","correct","MAP","BACK","NEXT","04"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["WORD CHOICE","Choose the right meaning of “get”","Discuss: Why is “get” less precise here?","Everyday: broad","• get information","• get a result","• get to a goal","Academic: precise","• receive information","• achieve a result","• achieve a goal","MAP","BACK","NEXT","05"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["ACADEMIC STYLE","Three habits of academic writing","Avoid contractions: don’t → do not; isn’t → is not.","Prefer objective phrasing: “The evidence suggests…” rather than “I think…”.","Give readers enough detail; do not rely on vague or conversational wording.","The experiment failed. → The experiment did not produce the expected results.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","06"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["PRACTISE","Quick upgrade: complete the sentences","Work individually first, then compare answers with a partner.","Participants ______ written information about the study.","Psychologists ______ to explain human behaviour.","Only 44% identified the ______ answer.","Companies use different ______ to attract customers.","The group ______ various methods.","CHECK YOUR ANSWERS","receive  •  attempt  •  correct  •  strategies  •  employed","MAP","BACK","NEXT","07"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["CONTEXT","A familiar word can shift meaning","TABLE","Everyday: furniture","Academic: information arranged in rows and columns","RIGHT","Everyday: opposite of left","In a text: correct / true; also a legal or moral claim","APPLY","In a study: use something","In another context: make a formal request","PICTURE","Everyday: an image","Specialised use in this unit: a container division","Read the whole sentence before choosing a definition.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","08"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["CONTEXT","Context detective","Explain which words in each sentence give you a clue.","The researchers applied a new method.","She applied for a place at university.","The third column of the table shows the figures.","Only 44% identified the right answer.","CHECK YOUR ANSWERS","apply = use  •  apply = make a formal request  •  table = organised data  •  right = correct","MAP","BACK","NEXT","09"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["PRACTISE","Rewrite it for an academic audience","In pairs, revise this sentence without changing its meaning:","“If you try to answer, you get feedback even if your answer isn’t quite right.”","USE THESE IDEAS","attempt   •   receive   •   correct   •   Avoid contractions","Write your revised sentence, then compare choices with another pair.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","10"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["PRACTISE","Exit ticket","One informal word and its formal equivalent: ______","One word whose meaning depends on context: ______","One academic sentence using receive, achieve, or attempt: ______","Complete independently before leaving.","MAP","BACK","11"]}],"2":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH 1     •     UNIT 02","Academic Study / Academic Life","University vocabulary • study profiles • higher education structure","INTERACTIVE CLASSROOM PRESENTATION","Discuss  •  Practise  •  Apply","01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 02  •  LEARNING MAP","Academic Study / Academic Life","Choose a section to jump in, or use NEXT to follow the lesson.","01","VOCABULARY","University essentials","02","STRUCTURE","How higher education fits together","03","SPEAKING","Talk about your studies","04","PRACTISE","Build a student profile","Tip: Switch to Slide Show mode and click a section card.","02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["TODAY’S LEARNING GOALS","By the end of this lesson, you can…","1","Use core vocabulary for university life.","2","Describe a course, subject, and qualification.","3","Explain how faculties and departments are organised.","4","Present a short personal academic profile.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","03"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["VOCABULARY","University essentials","Work with a partner: explain any terms you already know.","campus","→","university grounds and buildings","course","→","a programme of study","degree","→","an academic qualification","faculty","→","a major academic division","discipline","→","a field or subject of study","MAP","BACK","NEXT","04"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["STRUCTURE","From institution to subject","UNIVERSITY","higher education institution","→","FACULTY","major academic division","→","DEPARTMENT","organises teaching in a field","→","DISCIPLINE","subject area, e.g. economics","Which is broader: a faculty or a department? Explain.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","05"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["STRUCTURE","What is higher education?","Higher education is study after secondary school at a university or another higher education institution.","A course may lead to a qualification such as a BA or BSc degree.","Universities are often organised into faculties and departments.","A discipline is a subject area, such as law, history, or economics.","Turn to a partner: give one example of a discipline and a related department.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","06"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["SPEAKING","Talk about your studies","I’m studying ______.","I’m a student at ______ University.","I’m doing a course in ______.","My main subject / discipline is ______.","I’m an undergraduate / postgraduate student.","Choose two sentence frames and complete them with true information.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","07"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["SPEAKING","Read a student profile","“I’m Mohamed, a student at the University of Edinburgh. I’m in the final year of a three-year degree course. My main subjects are financial studies and computing.”","1. Where is Mohamed studying?","2. What stage of his course is he in?","3. Name his two subjects.","Find the words that signal institution, course stage, and subjects.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","08"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["PRACTISE","Choose the word that fits","Complete each sentence, then explain one choice.","She is doing a ______ in international law.","Acil is ______ law.","She is at ______ in Melbourne.","An ______ is studying for a first degree.","A degree programme is a ______ of study.","CHECK YOUR ANSWERS","course  •  studying  •  university  •  undergraduate  •  course","MAP","BACK","NEXT","09"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["PRACTISE","Spot the part of speech","Name the highlighted word’s part of speech.","different disciplines","academic institutions","a university degree","teaches courses","the history department","CHECK YOUR ANSWERS","discipline — noun  •  academic — adjective; institution — noun  •  degree — noun  •  teaches — verb; courses — noun  •  department — noun","MAP","BACK","NEXT","10"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["PRACTISE","Your 30-second academic profile","Prepare a short profile of four or five sentences. Include:","USE THESE IDEAS","your institution or intended institution   •   your course and main discipline   •   your study stage or qualification goal   •   at least three unit words","Share with a partner. Listen and ask one follow-up question.","MAP","BACK","11"]}],"3":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH 1     •     UNIT 03","Business Discipline","Business organisations • customers • products and services","INTERACTIVE CLASSROOM PRESENTATION","Discuss  •  Practise  •  Apply","01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 03  •  LEARNING MAP","Business Discipline","Choose a section to jump in, or use NEXT to follow the lesson.","01","ORGANISATIONS","Who does what?","02","LOCATION","Where should a business operate?","03","CUSTOMERS","Buyer, user, product, or service?","04","PRACTISE","Apply the vocabulary","Tip: Switch to Slide Show mode and click a section card.","02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["TODAY’S LEARNING GOALS","By the end of this lesson, you can…","1","Distinguish common business terms.","2","Explain why location matters to a business.","3","Tell the difference between a customer and a consumer.","4","Use business vocabulary to describe an organisation.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","03"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["ORGANISATIONS","Business, company, firm, industry","These words overlap, but they are not always interchangeable.","business","→","broad term for an organisation or commercial activity","company","→","an independent business organisation","firm","→","often a provider of professional services","industry","→","businesses and activities in a sector","Examples: family business • technology company • law firm • automobile industry","MAP","BACK","NEXT","04"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["LOCATION","Location is a business decision","Location can affect a business’s success and ability to compete.","Employers compare setup and ongoing costs before choosing a site.","Salary levels can differ between cities and regions.","Businesses may also need to consider access to customers, staff, and suppliers.","Think–pair–share: What kind of business benefits from a city-centre location?","MAP","BACK","NEXT","05"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["ORGANISATIONS","People at work: complete the idea","Use the word bank: employee • employer • professional • career","An ______ hires staff.","An ______ works for an organisation.","A marketing ______ brings specialised knowledge.","A ______ may include many different jobs over time.","CHECK YOUR ANSWERS","employer  •  employee  •  professional  •  career","MAP","BACK","NEXT","06"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["CUSTOMERS","Customer ≠ consumer","Discuss: Can one person be both customer and consumer?","CUSTOMER","• buys a product or service","• takes part in the transaction","• e.g. a parent buying a game","CONSUMER","• uses the product or service","• may be different from the buyer","• e.g. the child playing the game","MAP","BACK","NEXT","07"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["CUSTOMERS","Products, services, and promises","Match each term to its meaning.","product","→","something a business makes or sells","service","→","work or support offered to customers","purchase","→","buy something","guarantee","→","promise that something will be done or maintained","MAP","BACK","NEXT","08"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["CUSTOMERS","Why customers matter","Global markets and the internet have expanded consumer choice.","Access to product information can help people compare options.","Businesses focus on customer needs, experience, and quality.","Marketing helps businesses communicate value and compete.","Discuss: What information do you check before buying a product online?","MAP","BACK","NEXT","09"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["PRACTISE","Customer or consumer? Product or service?","Choose the best word and explain your reasoning.","A smartphone sold by Sony: ______","A child who uses a game bought by a parent: ______","Advice on doing business in Asia: ______","A repeat buyer at a shop: ______","CHECK YOUR ANSWERS","product  •  consumer  •  service  •  customer","MAP","BACK","NEXT","10"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["PRACTISE","Business case: make a recommendation","A small accountancy firm is choosing between a city-centre office and a lower-cost location outside the city.","USE THESE IDEAS","location   •   costs   •   professional   •   customer needs   •   industry","In pairs, recommend one location and give two reasons. Use at least three unit words.","MAP","BACK","11"]}],"4":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH 1     •     UNIT 04","Economics Discipline","The economy • GDP • finance • borrowing and lending","INTERACTIVE CLASSROOM PRESENTATION","Discuss  •  Practise  •  Apply","01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 04  •  LEARNING MAP","Economics Discipline","Choose a section to jump in, or use NEXT to follow the lesson.","01","THE ECONOMY","GDP and economic vocabulary","02","WORD FORMS","Build the word family","03","FINANCE","Borrow, lend, owe","04","PRACTISE","Make the calculation","Tip: Switch to Slide Show mode and click a section card.","02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["TODAY’S LEARNING GOALS","By the end of this lesson, you can…","1","Explain key terms about the economy and GDP.","2","Recognise common economics word forms.","3","Use borrow, lend, and owe correctly.","4","Distinguish profit from loss in simple examples.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","03"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["THE ECONOMY","What is the economy?","Economics studies how a country organises finance, trade, and industry.","GDP is the total value of goods and services produced in a country in a year.","GDP per person = total GDP ÷ population.","GDP per person is one indicator of economic output; it does not describe every aspect of well-being.","Which part of the GDP definition tells us the time period?","MAP","BACK","NEXT","04"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["THE ECONOMY","GDP per person: a simple calculation","Country’s GDP: 240 billion units","Population: 12 million people","GDP per person = 240 billion ÷ 12 million","= 20,000 units per person","With a partner: if GDP stays the same but population rises, what happens to GDP per person?","MAP","BACK","NEXT","05"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["WORD FORMS","Build the economics word family","Notice how the part of speech changes.","economy / economics","→","nouns","economic","→","adjective","finance","→","noun / verb","financial","→","adjective","invest","→","verb → investment (noun)","profit","→","noun / verb → profitable (adjective)","MAP","BACK","NEXT","06"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["WORD FORMS","Choose the form that fits","Business owners invest money to make more money.","You could take financial advice.","Analysts predict that economic growth will decline.","Online business can be more profitable.","Identify the part of speech of the bold idea in each sentence.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","07"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["FINANCE","Borrow, lend, or owe?","Memory cue: borrow from • lend to • owe to","BORROW / OWE","• borrow from a bank","• take money and agree to repay it","• owe money: have a debt to pay","LEND","• lend money to a borrower","• give money that must be repaid","• A bank lends; a customer borrows.","MAP","BACK","NEXT","08"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["FINANCE","How a bank loan works","BORROWER","applies for a loan","→","BANK","checks income and affordability","→","LOAN","money is lent at an interest rate","→","REPAY","borrower repays principal + interest","Who lends? Who borrows? What is interest?","MAP","BACK","NEXT","09"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["PRACTISE","Choose: borrow, lend, or owe","Complete each sentence. Pay attention to the direction of the money.","Banks ______ money to businesses.","If you ______ a large sum, plan how to repay it.","A company may ______ money in unpaid taxes.","The World Bank can ______ money to countries.","CHECK YOUR ANSWERS","lend  •  borrow  •  owe  •  lend","MAP","BACK","NEXT","10"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["PRACTISE","Profit or loss?","Calculate: result = revenue − costs. Is the result positive or negative?","EXAMPLE A","• Revenue: €8,000","• Costs: €6,500","• Result: €1,500 profit","EXAMPLE B","• Revenue: €5,000","• Costs: €5,800","• Result: €800 loss","MAP","BACK","NEXT","11"]},{"number":12,"paragraphs":["PRACTISE","Economics in a short paragraph","Write three or four sentences about the economy of a country you know, or about borrowing for a business.","USE THESE IDEAS","economy / economic   •   income / investment   •   borrow / lend / loan   •   profit / loss","Underline four unit words. Exchange paragraphs and check the vocabulary.","MAP","BACK","12"]}],"5":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH 1   •   UNIT 05","Introduction to Economics","Scarcity • choice • resources • present simple","Think like an economist. Explain your choices.","START","01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 05  •  LEARNING MAP","Choose your learning path","Click any card to jump to that section, or follow NEXT in lesson order.","01","THE BIG PICTURE","What economics studies","02","CHOICES","Scarcity & opportunity cost","03","THE ECONOMY","Goals & resources","04","LANGUAGE LAB","Vocabulary & grammar","Use Slide Show mode for clickable navigation.","02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["TODAY’S LEARNING GOALS","By the end of this lesson, you can…","1","Explain scarcity and opportunity cost with an example.","2","Distinguish macroeconomics from microeconomics.","3","Name the factors of production and the main economic goals.","4","Use key vocabulary and the present simple to describe economic facts.","03","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["01 • THINK","You have one limited resource: $10","Two toys cost $10 each. You can choose only one.","VOTE: Monopoly  •  Magic set  •  I need more information","Turn and tell a partner: What do you give up?","04","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["01 • CORE IDEA","What does economics study?","Economics studies how society manages scarce resources.","Production → distribution → consumption of goods and services","Point to one part of the cycle you see in everyday life.","05","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["02 • CHOICES","Scarcity makes every choice matter","SCARCITY","Wants exceed available time and resources.","OPPORTUNITY COST","The next best alternative you give up.","In the toy example, what is the opportunity cost of choosing the board game?","06","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["02 • PAIR CHALLENGE","A town has to make a choice","The town hires one more police officer instead of repairing several streets.","Discuss: What is chosen? What is given up? State the opportunity cost.","Be ready to explain your answer in one sentence.","07","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["03 • TWO LENSES","Macroeconomics vs microeconomics","MACRO","The economy as a whole\nor an entire industry","MICRO","Economic behaviour of\nindividual firms","Which lens would you use to study one local café? A country’s inflation?","08","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["03 • ECONOMIC GOALS","What outcomes do economies aim for?","MACRO POLICY","High employment","Price stability","Economic growth","MICRO GOALS","Efficiency","Equity","Freedom","Quick check: Which set focuses on employment and price levels?","09","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["03 • PRODUCTION","Four factors of production","LAND","Natural resources","LABOUR","Human effort","CAPITAL","Man-made production goods","ENTREPRENEURSHIP","Organises resources and takes risks","Output = goods and services","10","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["03 • LOOK CLOSER","What counts as a resource?","LAND","More than fields: water, minerals, timber, and building sites.","CAPITAL","Man-made goods used to produce other goods: tools, equipment, buildings, machinery.","LABOUR","The effort of anyone working in a business or organisation.","ENTREPRENEUR","Sets up a business, assembles resources, takes risks, and may earn profit or incur loss.","Which factor is a human resource? Which are material resources?","11","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":12,"paragraphs":["01 • READING JIGSAW","Become the expert on one paragraph","A","Scarcity & opportunity cost","Find the definition and both examples.","B","Macro goals","Find the three goals and explain one.","C","Micro goals","Find efficiency, equity, and freedom.","D","Factors of production","Name four inputs and one example each.","Read • prepare a 20-second explanation • teach your group","12","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":13,"paragraphs":["04 • VOCABULARY","Match the term to its meaning","distribution","sharing goods or income among people","scarce","available in limited quantity","objectives","goals or aims","entrepreneur","person who organises a business and takes risks","idle","not active or in use","equity","fairness","Pair task: cover the definitions, recall them, then switch roles.","13","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":14,"paragraphs":["04 • CHECK THE READING","True, false, or not given?","1.","Economics deals with production, distribution, and construction of goods and services.","T","F","NG","2.","People suffer when workers cannot find jobs and machinery is idle.","T","F","NG","3.","Microeconomics studies the behaviour of individual firms.","T","F","NG","4.","Land and capital are material resources.","T","F","NG","Answers: 1 F  •  2 T  •  3 T  •  4 T  •  5 T","The text says distribution and consumption; it does not say construction.","14","MAP","BACK","NEXT","5.","Economic systems reflect a country's history, traditions, aspirations, and politics.","T","F","NG"]},{"number":15,"paragraphs":["04 • GRAMMAR","Present simple: facts and regular patterns","I / WE / THEY","resources remain limited","HE / SHE / IT","scarcity occurs","Use the present simple for general facts, definitions, and repeated events.","Economics studies choices.  •  A firm invests.  •  People seek to satisfy wants.","Remember: a singular third-person subject usually adds -s / -es.","15","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":16,"paragraphs":["04 • GRAMMAR PRACTICE","Choose the correct present-simple form","An economy (achieve / achieves) its objectives.","achieves","Macroeconomics (investigate / investigates) the whole economy.","investigates","Scarcity (occur / occurs) when wants exceed resources.","occurs","Choices (involve / involves) opportunity costs.","involve","An entrepreneur (assemble / assembles) resources.","assembles","First answer alone; then justify the subject–verb agreement.","16","MAP","BACK","NEXT","1. An economy ______ its basic objectives.","Answer: achieves","2. Huge gaps ______ most people impoverished.","Answer: leave","3. Macroeconomics ______ resource allocation.","Answer: investigates","4. Scarcity ______ when wants exceed resources.","Answer: occurs","5. Scarcity ______ that choices have costs.","Answer: implies","6. A town ______ an extra police officer.","Answer: hires","7. Scarcity ______ us to choose.","Answer: forces","8. An economic system ______ a country's history.","Answer: reflects","9. An inefficient economy ______ resources.","Answer: wastes","10. Maximum freedom ______ people to have wide choices.","Answer: requires","Read the subject first; then check singular or plural agreement."]},{"number":17,"paragraphs":["FINAL CHALLENGE","Explain an economic choice","A community has limited funds. It can repair a bridge or build a new clinic.","What is scarce?","What are the alternatives?","What might be the opportunity cost?","Which factors of production are needed?","Use at least three unit terms and one present-simple sentence.","17","MAP","BACK"]}],"6":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH COURSE  •  FIRST-YEAR UNIVERSITY  •  UNIT 06","Money","Money in an economic system • vocabulary • simple present passive","Money makes exchange easier by giving buyers and sellers a shared means of payment.","LEARN  •  DISCUSS  •  APPLY","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 06  •  LEARNING MAP","Money","Choose a section to jump to, or follow the lesson with NEXT.","01","EXCHANGE","02","FUNCTIONS","03","LANGUAGE","04","APPLY","For clickable navigation, present the deck in Slide Show mode.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["LEARNING OBJECTIVES","By the end of this lecture, you can…","1","Explain why money is useful in complex exchange.","2","Describe the three main functions of money.","3","Distinguish barter, commodity money, and fiat money.","4","Form and recognize the simple present passive.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  03","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["OPENING QUESTION","THINK • VOTE • EXPLAIN","Could you trade your way through the day?","You have bread but need a bus ride. The driver wants a notebook, not bread. What would make this exchange easier?","A","Find someone who wants bread","B","Agree on a common means of payment","C","Trade several goods until both sides agree","30 seconds think • 60 seconds pair discussion • whole-class vote","There may be more than one defensible answer—support yours with a reason.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  04","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["CORE CONCEPTS","The essential ideas","MEDIUM OF EXCHANGE","Accepted to buy goods and services.","STORE OF VALUE","Can be kept and used later, subject to changes in purchasing power.","UNIT / MEASURE OF ACCOUNT","Allows prices and debts to be stated in a common unit.","FIAT MONEY","Accepted as legal tender and supported by institutions and public confidence.","Check: explain one card to a partner without reading it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  05","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["COMPARE & CONNECT","Barter and money solve different problems","BARTER","Goods are exchanged directly.","A coincidence of wants is needed.","Finding a fair exchange rate can take time.","MONEY ECONOMY","Money is accepted by many participants.","Prices are stated in a shared unit.","Exchange and saving are more convenient.","Pair-share: give a real example of each side of this comparison.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  06","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["ACADEMIC VOCABULARY","Terms you will use in this unit","barter","direct exchange without money","legal tender","money recognized for payment by law","currency","the money used in a country","transaction","an act of buying, selling, or exchanging","medium of exchange","something accepted in trade","store of value","a way to transfer purchasing power over time","Active recall: cover the right-hand side and define each term aloud.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  07","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["LANGUAGE FOCUS","Simple present passive: focus on the process","Subject + am / is / are + past participle","Money is accepted as payment.","Prices are stated in dinars.","Deposits are held in bank accounts.","Notice the form • identify the meaning • create a new example.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  08","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["GUIDED PRACTICE","Try first; then check","1. Money ______ as a medium of exchange. (use)","is used","2. Prices ______ in a common unit. (state)","are stated","3. Fiat money ______ as legal tender. (define)","is defined","4. Banks ______ by customers to hold deposits. (use)","are used","Suggested answers are shown for self-check after discussion.","Teacher move: ask students to justify one answer, not only supply it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  09","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["FORMATIVE CHECK","Pause and retrieve","1","Which function lets us compare prices?","CHECK: Unit / measure of account","2","What is the central difficulty of barter?","CHECK: Coincidence of wants","3","Complete: Money ___ accepted in most transactions.","CHECK: is","Students answer on paper / mini-whiteboards before revealing the check.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  10","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["COLLABORATIVE APPLICATION","Design a local exchange system","Your campus club wants to exchange tutoring, printing, meals, and transport support among students.","1","Identify two problems of direct barter.","2","Propose a shared unit or payment method.","3","Explain how it functions as a medium, store, and measure.","Roles: facilitator • vocabulary tracker • reporter","Report one conclusion and one question your group still has.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  11","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":12,"paragraphs":["EXIT TICKET","Three things to take away","1","Money reduces exchange frictions.","2","Its core functions are medium of exchange, store of value, and unit of account.","3","Present passive: am/is/are + past participle.","Before you leave: write one concept, one new term, and one question.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 06  •  12","MAP","BACK"]}],"7":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH COURSE  •  FIRST-YEAR UNIVERSITY  •  UNIT 07","Markets","Demand • supply • curve shifts • simple present passive","A market connects buyers and sellers; it need not be a physical place.","LEARN  •  DISCUSS  •  APPLY","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 07  •  LEARNING MAP","Markets","Choose a section to jump to, or follow the lesson with NEXT.","01","MARKET","02","DEMAND","03","SUPPLY","04","LANGUAGE","For clickable navigation, present the deck in Slide Show mode.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["LEARNING OBJECTIVES","By the end of this lecture, you can…","1","Define a market and describe price coordination.","2","Explain the laws of demand and supply.","3","Distinguish movement along a curve from a shift of the curve.","4","Use the simple present passive to describe market processes.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  03","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["OPENING QUESTION","THINK • VOTE • EXPLAIN","Why do strawberry prices change?","A late frost reduces the strawberry harvest just as more customers arrive at the market. Predict what may happen to price and quantity exchanged.","A","Price falls; quantity rises","B","Price rises; available quantity falls","C","Nothing changes","30 seconds think • 60 seconds pair discussion • whole-class vote","There may be more than one defensible answer—support yours with a reason.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  04","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["CORE CONCEPTS","The essential ideas","MARKET","Buyers and sellers interact to exchange goods or services.","DEMAND","At a lower price, consumers generally demand a greater quantity, other things equal.","SUPPLY","At a higher price, producers generally offer a greater quantity, other things equal.","PRICE SIGNAL","Price changes coordinate consumer choices and producer decisions.","Check: explain one card to a partner without reading it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  05","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["COMPARE & CONNECT","Read the two market relationships","DEMAND","Price ↓ → quantity demanded ↑","Inverse relationship; curve slopes down.","Non-price factors can shift demand.","SUPPLY","Price ↑ → quantity supplied ↑","Direct relationship; curve slopes up.","Costs and technology can shift supply.","Pair-share: give a real example of each side of this comparison.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  06","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["ACADEMIC VOCABULARY","Terms you will use in this unit","quantity demanded","amount consumers will buy at a given price","quantity supplied","amount producers will offer at a given price","demand schedule","table of price and quantities demanded","substitute","alternative good used instead","marginal utility","additional satisfaction from one more unit","ceteris paribus","other relevant factors held constant","Active recall: cover the right-hand side and define each term aloud.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  07","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["LANGUAGE FOCUS","Simple present passive in academic explanations","am / is / are + past participle; use it when the process or result matters more than the agent.","The market price is determined by demand and supply.","Market demand is calculated by summing individual quantities.","Goods are supplied at different prices.","Notice the form • identify the meaning • create a new example.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  08","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["GUIDED PRACTICE","Try first; then check","1. The market price ______ by buyers and sellers. (determine)","is determined","2. Individual quantities ______ to find market demand. (sum)","are summed","3. Goods ______ at each price. (supply)","are supplied","4. A demand schedule ______ as a table or graph. (express)","is expressed","Suggested answers are shown for self-check after discussion.","Teacher move: ask students to justify one answer, not only supply it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  09","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["FORMATIVE CHECK","Pause and retrieve","1","Price falls: what usually happens to quantity demanded?","CHECK: It rises","2","Does a change in the good’s own price shift its curve?","CHECK: No; it causes movement along the curve","3","Name one non-price determinant of demand.","CHECK: Income, tastes, number of consumers, related prices, or expectations","Students answer on paper / mini-whiteboards before revealing the check.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  10","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["COLLABORATIVE APPLICATION","Market noticeboard simulation","A popular café introduces a student discount. Nearby, the price of coffee beans rises and a new café opens.","1","Decide which event affects demand and which affects supply.","2","For each event, say movement or curve shift.","3","Predict the likely direction of pressure on price; explain any uncertainty.","Roles: facilitator • vocabulary tracker • reporter","Report one conclusion and one question your group still has.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  11","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":12,"paragraphs":["EXIT TICKET","Three things to take away","1","Demand generally slopes down; supply generally slopes up.","2","Own-price changes cause movement along a curve; non-price determinants shift it.","3","Passive voice describes how market outcomes are determined.","Before you leave: write one concept, one new term, and one question.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 07  •  12","MAP","BACK"]}],"8":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH COURSE  •  FIRST-YEAR UNIVERSITY  •  UNIT 08","Size of the Economy","GDP • real and nominal values • the business cycle • phrasal verbs","GDP measures output, but no single statistic captures every dimension of well-being.","LEARN  •  DISCUSS  •  APPLY","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 08  •  LEARNING MAP","Size of the Economy","Choose a section to jump to, or follow the lesson with NEXT.","01","GDP","02","COMPARE","03","LANGUAGE","04","APPLY","For clickable navigation, present the deck in Slide Show mode.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["LEARNING OBJECTIVES","By the end of this lecture, you can…","1","Define GDP and explain what it includes.","2","Distinguish nominal GDP from real GDP and calculate GDP per capita.","3","Interpret the business cycle and recognize limits of GDP.","4","Use common phrasal verbs from economic contexts.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  03","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["OPENING QUESTION","THINK • VOTE • EXPLAIN","A bigger economy: better life for everyone?","Country A has a larger total GDP than Country B, but also has a much larger population. Which country has higher output per person?","A","Always Country A","B","Compare GDP per capita","C","GDP cannot be compared","30 seconds think • 60 seconds pair discussion • whole-class vote","There may be more than one defensible answer—support yours with a reason.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  04","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["CORE CONCEPTS","The essential ideas","GDP","Value of final goods and services produced within a country during a period.","FINAL OUTPUT","Count final goods and services; avoid double-counting intermediate inputs.","REAL GDP","Output value adjusted for price changes using a base year.","GDP PER CAPITA","GDP divided by population; useful for cross-country comparison, not a complete welfare measure.","Check: explain one card to a partner without reading it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  05","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["COMPARE & CONNECT","Make GDP figures comparable","NOMINAL GDP","Uses current prices.","Can rise because prices rise.","Not directly comparable across inflationary years.","REAL GDP","Adjusts for changes in prices.","Better for comparing output over time.","Uses a price index and base year.","Pair-share: give a real example of each side of this comparison.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  06","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["ACADEMIC VOCABULARY","Terms you will use in this unit","final good","output bought for final use","intermediate good","input used to produce another good","nominal","measured in current prices","real","adjusted for inflation","recession","significant decline in economic activity","per capita","for each person; total divided by population","Active recall: cover the right-hand side and define each term aloud.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  07","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["LANGUAGE FOCUS","Phrasal verbs: verb + particle","A phrasal verb combines a verb with an adverb or preposition. The particle can change the meaning.","add up the values = calculate the total (transitive)","GDP can go up over time = increase (intransitive)","Prices may drop down / fall (intransitive)","Notice the form • identify the meaning • create a new example.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  08","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["GUIDED PRACTICE","Try first; then check","1. To find total GDP, ______ the values. (add up)","add up","2. Real GDP is found by ______ nominal values for inflation. (adjust)","adjusting","3. A recession occurs when output ______ significantly. (go down)","goes down","4. GDP per capita is calculated by ______ GDP by population. (divide)","dividing","Suggested answers are shown for self-check after discussion.","Teacher move: ask students to justify one answer, not only supply it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  09","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["FORMATIVE CHECK","Pause and retrieve","1","Does GDP count intermediate goods separately?","CHECK: No, to avoid double counting","2","What adjustment helps compare output across years?","CHECK: Inflation adjustment / real GDP","3","GDP = 600 billion; population = 30 million. GDP per capita?","CHECK: 20,000 per person","Students answer on paper / mini-whiteboards before revealing the check.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  10","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["COLLABORATIVE APPLICATION","GDP detective","A country’s nominal GDP rises by 8%, while average prices rise by 6%. Its population also grows.","1","Explain why nominal growth alone may overstate output growth.","2","State why real GDP is useful.","3","Name two things GDP does not directly measure.","Roles: facilitator • vocabulary tracker • reporter","Report one conclusion and one question your group still has.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  11","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":12,"paragraphs":["EXIT TICKET","Three things to take away","1","GDP measures final output within national borders over a period.","2","Real GDP adjusts for prices; GDP per capita adjusts for population.","3","GDP is an indicator, not a complete measure of well-being.","Before you leave: write one concept, one new term, and one question.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 08  •  12","MAP","BACK"]}],"9":[{"number":1,"paragraphs":["ENGLISH COURSE  •  FIRST-YEAR UNIVERSITY  •  UNIT 09","Management","Management levels • responsibilities • modal verbs","Management coordinates people and resources so an organization can achieve its goals.","LEARN  •  DISCUSS  •  APPLY","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  01"]},{"number":2,"paragraphs":["UNIT 09  •  LEARNING MAP","Management","Choose a section to jump to, or follow the lesson with NEXT.","01","LEVELS","02","RESPONSIBILITIES","03","LANGUAGE","04","CASE","For clickable navigation, present the deck in Slide Show mode.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  02"]},{"number":3,"paragraphs":["LEARNING OBJECTIVES","By the end of this lecture, you can…","1","Define management and identify common managerial skills.","2","Compare low-, middle-, and senior-management responsibilities.","3","Use modal verbs to describe duties, advice, ability, and possibility.","4","Apply management vocabulary to a short workplace case.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  03","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":4,"paragraphs":["OPENING QUESTION","THINK • VOTE • EXPLAIN","Who should solve this problem?","A customer complaint reveals a repeated delivery delay. Who should act first, who should coordinate a fix, and who may change company policy?","A","Front line, middle, senior","B","Senior, front line, middle","C","Only the CEO","30 seconds think • 60 seconds pair discussion • whole-class vote","There may be more than one defensible answer—support yours with a reason.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  04","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":5,"paragraphs":["CORE CONCEPTS","The essential ideas","LOWER / FRONT-LINE","Supervises everyday work, quality, feedback, and immediate staff issues.","MIDDLE","Translates strategy into departmental plans and guides front-line managers.","SENIOR","Sets overall direction, strategy, and company-wide policy; accountable for results.","MANAGERIAL SKILLS","Planning, communication, organization, leadership, and resource coordination.","Check: explain one card to a partner without reading it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  05","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":6,"paragraphs":["COMPARE & CONNECT","Management levels: connect strategy to daily work","STRATEGIC DIRECTION","Senior managers set goals and policy.","Middle managers communicate plans and coordinate implementation.","Decisions affect the whole organization.","DAILY OPERATIONS","Front-line managers guide employees.","They monitor work quality and provide feedback.","Problems move through appropriate channels.","Pair-share: give a real example of each side of this comparison.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  06","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":7,"paragraphs":["ACADEMIC VOCABULARY","Terms you will use in this unit","strategy","plan designed to achieve a long-term aim","mission","organization’s core purpose","supervise","direct and monitor work","feedback","information about performance","accountable","responsible for actions and results","shareholder","owner of shares in a company","Active recall: cover the right-hand side and define each term aloud.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  07","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":8,"paragraphs":["LANGUAGE FOCUS","Modal verbs for management responsibilities","modal + base verb (no -s, no “to”): must, should, can, may.","Managers must communicate goals. (obligation)","Supervisors should give useful feedback. (advice)","Middle managers can coordinate departments. (ability)","Notice the form • identify the meaning • create a new example.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  08","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":9,"paragraphs":["GUIDED PRACTICE","Try first; then check","1. Senior leaders ______ set the overall direction. (obligation)","must","2. Managers ______ provide regular feedback. (advice)","should","3. A clear strategy ______ improve coordination. (possibility)","may / can","4. A supervisor can ______ work quality. (monitor)","monitor","Suggested answers are shown for self-check after discussion.","Teacher move: ask students to justify one answer, not only supply it.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  09","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":10,"paragraphs":["FORMATIVE CHECK","Pause and retrieve","1","Which level communicates strategic goals to front-line managers?","CHECK: Middle management","2","Who oversees employees’ everyday work?","CHECK: Low-level / front-line management","3","After “must”, which verb form is used?","CHECK: Base form","Students answer on paper / mini-whiteboards before revealing the check.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  10","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":11,"paragraphs":["COLLABORATIVE APPLICATION","Management escalation","A department misses a target because of a scheduling problem and a training gap.","1","Assign one action to each management level.","2","Use must, should, and can in your recommendations.","3","Identify one measure that would show improvement.","Roles: facilitator • vocabulary tracker • reporter","Report one conclusion and one question your group still has.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  11","MAP","BACK","NEXT"]},{"number":12,"paragraphs":["EXIT TICKET","Three things to take away","1","Management coordinates people, tasks, and resources.","2","Senior sets direction; middle translates; front line supervises daily work.","3","Modal + base verb expresses obligation, advice, ability, or possibility.","Before you leave: write one concept, one new term, and one question.","ENGLISH 1  •  UNIT 09  •  12","MAP","BACK"]}]};
